        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 41 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 34 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 29 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The tutor's video feed is removed, and the student's video feed is enlarged.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Jordan test has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Jordan has joined\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Jordan has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 37 seconds]\nTurn 1. STUDENT: water.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: Hi.\nTurn 3. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 4. TUTOR: How are you doing?\nTurn 5. STUDENT: Good.\nTurn 6. TUTOR: Good.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Ooh, I have to show you something really cool that I just found out.\nTurn 8. STUDENT: What?\nTurn 9. TUTOR: There's like a leaderboard thing that we have for our on our um, math website.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: So,\nTurn 11. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - sharing screen] Tutor shares her screen. The screen is black with a blue loading circle in the center.\nTurn 11. TUTOR: you're right here. And so,\nTurn 12. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen loads to a website called \"MathQuest\". It shows a \"Class League\" leaderboard. The student, Jordan M., is in second place with 15 points. Kimberly L. is in first with 16 points. Annabelle S. is in third with 13 points. A text box says \"Class League. Get 2x flame to overtake Kimberly L.! Take your next topic\".\nTurn 12. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - scrolling] Tutor scrolls down the page, showing a grid of other students' avatars and names.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Ooh, do you want to ch Do you wanna change your picture?\nTurn 13. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating website] Tutor clicks on the student's avatar icon at the top right, then clicks on the student's name, which takes her to the \"Your Account\" page. It shows the student's name \"Jordan M. At Bright Futures\" and username \"<Username>\".\nTurn 13. STUDENT: Maybe.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - navigating website] Tutor clicks on the \"Shop\" icon (a shopping cart) at the top of the page. The page loads and then shows a \"Shop\" with \"Avatars\", \"Prizes\", and \"Themes\" tabs. The \"Avatars\" tab is selected, showing many different avatar options.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: You can do this afterwards. You can we can do the the schoolwork first and if you're like,