26. TUTOR: Do you need help?\nTurn 27. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: Okay. So, I'm not sure. Okay. So, we're trying to find the total. So, we have fifteen birds and twelve birds. So, what do you think we're going to do with those two numbers? Are we going to add them or subtract them? We're trying to find the total. So, we have fifteen birds and then twelve more birds come. So, we're going to add them. So, we're looking for fifteen plus twelve equals question mark. So, which one of these looks like fifteen plus twelve equals question mark?\nTurn 29. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicks check] Tutor clicks the \"Check\" button.\nTurn 29. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen shows \"A is correct!\" with an explanation. The tutor clicks the \"Next\" button.\nTurn 29. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p2 - 'A shop has 13 green apples and 37 red apples. Which equation can be used to find the total number of apples?' (start)] The problem is displayed on the screen. It reads: \"A shop has 13 green apples and 37 red apples. Which equation can be used to find the total number of apples?\" Below the text are four equations labeled A, B, C, and D.\nA: 13 + [box] = 37\nB: [box] + 13 = 37\nC: 13 + 37 = [box]\nD: 37 + [box] = 13\nTurn 29. STUDENT: Um, I think I know.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 67 seconds] Tutor's cursor points to option A.\nTutor's cursor moves between the numbers in option A.\nTurn 30. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 31. STUDENT: Um, A?\nTurn 32. TUTOR: A? So you think thirteen plus something equals thirty-seven?\nTurn 33. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: Why do you think that? Why do you think thirteen plus something equals thirty-seven?\nTurn 35. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 11 seconds]\nTurn 35. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 10 seconds]\nTurn 36. TUTOR: So what are we trying to find?\nTurn 37. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 37. STUDENT: I need help.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nFor this next reply, decide privately which of the following teaching policies fits the current learner evidence, then apply it. Use the current mathematical task and recent student work in the context of the full conversation. Agreement with a tutor-supplied answer is not proof of understanding. An independently corrected earlier error does not by itself justify more help.\nSCAFFOLDING: Provide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nRIGOR: Push for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
